From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Fri, 10 Jun 94 21:47:41 GMT
Subject: [#94-6-22] BOOK: The Ascent of Mind by William Calvin

>  The Ascent of Mind: Ice Age Climates and the Evolution of Intelligence,
>by William H. Calvin (wcalvin@u.washington.edu), Bantam Books 1991, is an
>interesting set of hypotheses which try to explain the unusual increase in
>human brain sizes over the past 2.5 million years.

I'd like to add a couple of points from a TV programme I saw a while back,
on the importance of bipedalism to brain evolution.  Since bipedalism left
the hands free to develop tools etc, this fed back to the brain.  Those who
engaged in tasks which developed dexterity were exercising their brains
more compared to the quadrupeds who were unable to, and this promoted an
increase in brain size. 

But bigger brains consume more power, requiring a more efficient cooling
system (this evolution occurred in the East Rift valley of Africa).  An
engine can only be as large as the radiator allows.  Luckily, walking
upright confers an increased cooling efficiency.  The taller, thinner body
maximises surface area compared to volume, and minimises the area heated by
the overhead sun.  Also, the head now being further above the ground
encounters higher wind speeds at that altitude.  Bipeds lost about one
and a third times the body heat of quadrupeds, and required less water.  So
this starts speeding up the process of brain evolution, then Calvin's
explanation can take over -- the bipeds invent their killer frisbees and
obtain a clearly higher fitness, and the ice age cycles play their part.

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

