On Fri, 10 Jun 1994, T. David Burns wrote:

> At  5:07 AM 6/1/94 -0400, James Daugherty quoted Marvin Minsky:
> > when A says
> >> that "P is an emergent phenomenon, A means that "I do not see how P is a
> >> predictable possible consequence of the elements, interactions, and initial
> >> conditions of system S".  In other words "emergence" is a sense of surprise
> >> in an observer, not a mysterious propert of a system.  Science -- that is,
> >> reductionism -- proceeds by examining things until one understand enough to
> >> be no longer surprised.
> 
> I need some sort of term to express this stuff (ant colonies, convection
> cells, informal institutions, etc.). Complex phenomena, collective
> phenomena, mass phenomena, systems, spontaneous orders, organisms, all
> these terms seem inadequate. I liked 'emergent phenomena' when I first
> heard it, but more and more people seem to put a mystical connotation on
> this. So what should I use instead? Or is this category just not useful?
> 
> -- Dave                       tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
> --                                (609) 921-2499 (fax or voice message)
> 

In spite of the reductionist critic there is nothing mystical or "in the 
eye of the beholder" about emergent phenomena.

In fact, it is a purposeful (IMHO) distortion of the concept to build a 
"straw man".

Emergent phenomena only make real sense in the context of an Aristotelian 
conception of reality consisting of form and matter.  The new level of 
complexity predictably results in a new entity with new form.

Rupert Sheldrake explains it best in his The Presence of the Past:  
Morphic resonance and the Habits of Nature.

I plan to post some of his arguments to encourage the reading of his book 
among extropians.  Frankly, I think The Selfish Gene represents carrying 
the reductionist fallacy all the way to absurdity.


