From: Mark Grant <Mark.Grant@isltd.insignia.com>
Date: Sat, 11 Jun 94 20:13:43 BST
Subject: [#94-6-28] Digital Cash & Free Banking

-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr) said :

> I've managed to engage Lawrence White (author of Competition and Currency
> as well as other academic articles on free banking) in some e-mail
> correspondence on digital cash and free banking.  He says he read Hal
> Finney's article in Extropy! on digital cash, but has some questions about
> connecting, say, an offshore digital bank to an ATM network - not whether
> it's technically possible, but whether the Feds are likely to regulate
> against it, 

I doubt that they'd be terribly happy with the idea, and it seems to me
that they'd probably start flooding the media with stories about
electronic money-laundering, tax-evasion, etc (though whether that
would be seen as good or bad publicity is unclear 8-)). I suspect that
they'd try hard to stop it, or at least to ensure that they could find
out who used the ATMs, and how much they withdrew.

> and whether such regulations would be enforceable. 

I think it depends to a large extent on how many 'physical' (i.e. non
digital-cash) customers the bank already had. It would seem better to
start with a bank that's already connected to the ATM network in some
form (if only by issuing credit cards) and then add digital cash than
to try to start a new digital-cash-only bank and get it connected. If
the bank already has plenty of rich, vocal customers whose cards would
be stopped by the regulations, then they're much more likely to be able
to defend against those regulations than if they only had 'tax-evading',
'money-laundering' digital customers.

There are plenty of other solutions, involving moving the money from
the offshore digital bank to another bank before you withdraw it, but
they're not exactly convenient (though if you could arrange with
another offshore bank that isn't blocked to give out ATM cards to your
customers and automatically pass requests on to your digital bank when
they were used, it would probably work reasonably well - until the
other bank was blocked too).

In the very worst case, you would presumably end up in a situation
where you'd have to find someone somewhere who'd act as a 'virtual-ATM'
by buying your digital cash for 'real' cash. Since anyone with a net
link could do that, it would be much harder to prevent than the
use of 'real' ATM machines.

Overall, I think that they can certainly prevent widespread
availability of ATMs that accept cards for your offshore digital bank,
but I doubt that they can stop it entirely. Fortunately, from what I've
read of past banking methods, more devious minds than my own can
probably come up with many better ideas....


				Mark


P.S. Reading 'Applied Cryptography' a few days ago, Bruce Shneier
claims that there is a method that allows you to use digital cash just
like 'real' cash, without all that messing around with the bank after
each transaction.  However, he doesn't give any details other than a
reference to a paper by some Japanese guys, and the mention that each
'note' is 20k in size...

